# 🛡️ Module 10 — Identity & Access Management (IAM) and Active Directory

> Understanding how organizations manage digital identities, authenticate users, control access, and secure enterprise environments using Identity & Access Management and Microsoft Active Directory.

## 📌 Module Overview

Identity and Access Management (IAM) is a fundamental part of cybersecurity. It ensures that the right users have the right access to the right resources at the right time.

In this module, I explored how organizations manage user identities, authenticate employees, assign permissions, administer Active Directory environments, and monitor suspicious identity-related activities.

I also learned how SOC analysts investigate authentication attacks, privilege escalation, unauthorized access, and compromised accounts.

---

## 📚 Topics Covered

### 1. 🔐 Identity & Access Management (IAM)
- Understanding IAM and its importance.
- Identification, Authentication, Authorization, and Accountability.
- Identity lifecycle management.
- Access governance.

### 2. 🔑 Authentication Mechanisms
- Password-based authentication.
- Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA).
- Single Sign-On (SSO).
- Identity Federation.
- Modern and passwordless authentication.

### 3. 🛡️ Authorization Models
- Role-Based Access Control (RBAC).
- Principle of Least Privilege.
- Access management and permission assignment.
- Privilege creep.
- Vertical and horizontal privilege escalation.

### 4. 🏢 Active Directory Fundamentals
- Understanding Microsoft Active Directory.
- Domains and Domain Controllers.
- Organizational Units (OUs).
- Group Policy Objects (GPOs).
- Centralized identity and resource management.

### 5. ⚙️ Active Directory Administration
- User account management.
- Security and distribution groups.
- Access control and permissions.
- Password and account lockout policies.
- Administrative security practices.

### 6. 🔒 Active Directory Security
- Privileged accounts and administrative access.
- Credential security.
- Kerberos authentication awareness.
- Account monitoring.
- Security baselines and attack surface awareness.

### 7. 📊 Identity Monitoring
- Login events and authentication logs.
- Access monitoring.
- Suspicious authentication activities.
- Windows Security Event IDs.
- SOC investigation workflows.

### 8. 🤖 AI-Assisted Identity Security
- AI-assisted authentication analysis.
- Access review and identity assessments.
- Investigation support.
- Validating AI-generated findings and recommendations.

### 9. 🧪 Hands-on Applications
- Analyzing authentication logs.
- Reviewing access control scenarios.
- Investigating suspicious login activities.
- Assessing identity security risks.
- Creating IAM security reports.

---

## ⚡ Quick Revision

| Concept | Key Point |
|---|---|
| IAM | Manages digital identities and controls access. |
| Identification | Claims an identity. |
| Authentication | Verifies an identity. |
| Authorization | Determines what an authenticated user can access. |
| Accountability | Tracks and records user activities. |
| MFA | Uses two or more different authentication factor categories. |
| SSO | Allows access to multiple applications through one authentication process. |
| Federation | Establishes trust between different identity systems. |
| RBAC | Assigns permissions through roles. |
| Least Privilege | Provides only the minimum permissions required. |
| Active Directory | Centrally manages users, computers, groups, and resources. |
| Domain Controller | Provides AD authentication and directory services. |
| OU | Organizes AD objects and supports delegation and Group Policy linking. |
| GPO | Applies configuration and security policies. |
| Kerberos | Ticket-based network authentication protocol. |
| Privilege Escalation | Gaining higher or unauthorized privileges. |
| Identity Monitoring | Tracks authentication and access activities. |

### 🖥️ Important Windows Event IDs

| Event ID | Description |
|---|---|
| 4624 | Successful logon |
| 4625 | Failed logon |
| 4634 | Account logged off |
| 4648 | Logon attempted using explicit credentials |
| 4672 | Special privileges assigned to a new logon |
| 4720 | User account created |
| 4722 | User account enabled |
| 4726 | User account deleted |
| 4728 | Member added to a global security group |
| 4732 | Member added to a local security group |
| 4740 | User account locked out |
| 4663 | Attempt to access an audited object |

---

## 🚨 Important Security Threats

| Threat | Description |
|---|---|
| Brute Force | Trying many passwords against one account. |
| Password Spraying | Trying a few common passwords against multiple accounts. |
| Credential Stuffing | Using previously leaked credentials against other services. |
| Privilege Escalation | Obtaining higher privileges than authorized. |
| Privilege Creep | Accumulation of unnecessary permissions over time. |
| Account Compromise | Unauthorized access to a legitimate user account. |
| Unauthorized Access | Accessing resources without proper permission. |

---

## 🔍 SOC Investigation Workflow

A SOC analyst investigates identity-related security incidents using a structured process.

```text
       Alert Detection
              |
              ▼
         Alert Triage
              |
              ▼
         Investigation
              |
              ▼
          Containment
              |
              ▼
           Recovery
              |
              ▼
         Documentation
```

Important investigation activities:

- Analyze authentication logs and Windows Event IDs.
- Investigate suspicious IP addresses and login timestamps.
- Identify unusual login patterns.
- Review unexpected permission and group membership changes.
- Investigate compromised accounts.
- Revoke unauthorized access and sessions.
- Document findings and recommend security improvements.

